\description{
A step-1 builder of the fishing-activity flow: the port calls, the times a vessel spends in port. Port calls
and voyages (\code{\link[=rb_create_voyages]{rb_create_voyages()}}) are the two halves of a track, and the two functions share their methods.
}
\details{
\itemize{
\item \code{"dwell"}: a port call is elapsed time at a port, in port for at least a minimum time (\code{min_dwell_h}), from
any of three rules on the pings and, optionally, from declared port events (below). \code{\link[=rb_create_voyages]{rb_create_voyages()}}
with \code{method = "dwell"} cuts the track at these port calls.
\item \code{"runs"}, \code{"jepol"}, \code{"cnr"}, \code{"iapesca"}: a port call is the time in port between two voyages of the
method (\code{\link[=rb_create_voyages]{rb_create_voyages()}}, whose arguments for these methods go in \code{...}), from the end of one voyage
to the start of the next, at the port the first ended in, else the port the second began from. Where
neither voyage reaches a port (a long voyage split, or a silence at sea), there is no port call between
them, nor before a vessel's first voyage or after its last.
}

The rules of \code{"dwell"}:
\itemize{
\item \code{tag_then_gap}: two consecutive pings at the same port, the first within \code{radius_in} of it, at least
the minimum dwell apart, one of them inside the polygon;
\item \code{gap_near_port}: the same, neither inside the polygon;
\item \code{tag_span}: a run of pings inside the polygon spanning at least the minimum dwell;
\item \code{event_pair} (with \code{events}): an arrival then a departure event at the same port, adjacent in the
vessel's events, both judged good, at least the minimum dwell apart, and no ping between them farther than
\code{pair_away_m} from the port (the track shows the vessel left: a pair whose events between were lost).
}

With \code{speed_kn} (fishycode plan 031 §4.1), two rules scale the minimum with the ping interval, as a time
budget: the time the vessel could have spent in port, given the pings either side and the time to sail from
and to them.
\itemize{
\item \code{gap_budget}: two consecutive pings, the first within \code{radius_in} of a port, the second anywhere: a port call
when the time between them, less the sailing time from the first ping to the port and from the port to the
second, is at least the minimum dwell. Where pings are sparse, the second is often outside the departure
radius that \code{tag_then_gap} asks for.
\item \code{span_budget}: a run of pings inside the polygon, too short for \code{tag_span}: a port call when the time from
the ping before the run to the ping after it, less the sailing time to and from the port, is at least the
minimum dwell. With hourly pings a call of three hours holds only two or three pings.
